  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 342 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThursday night into Friday, we begin to see the upper high slide \nwest towards Texas, pushing the ridge above it north through the \neastern Plains and Midwest. Northwest flow, ahead of the ridge, will \npush a surface high through the Great Lakes region behind a cold \nfront trying to work its way south through the CWA, which could \nbring northeast winds to southern Indiana and central Kentucky. This \nwill likely increase rain chances to 20-30 percent. Models have been \nconsistent with this feature for several days, so believe it could \nprovide some bust potential with extending the heat advisory into \nFriday and/or Saturday. \n\nWith that said, by Saturday, the front is expected to begin moving \nback north as a warm front. WAA keeps high temperatures in the low \nto mid 90s through the weekend before upper 80s return on Monday for \nsome. There is a general trend in precipitation chances increasing \nto 20-40 percent during the beginning to middle of next week as \ndifferent waves work through the messy zonal pattern, but consensus \nis weak.",
